meta {
  name: confirm-image
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/admin/v1/menu/items/{{admin_item_uid}}/image
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "object_key": "{{image_object_key}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("responds 200, or refuses an upload that never happened", function () {
    // 422 is the expected result of a sequential collection run: upload-image-url minted a
    // key, but nothing PUT any bytes to it, so there is no object to attach. Verifying that
    // refusal is worth more than skipping the request.
    expect([200, 422, 501]).to.include(res.status)
  })

  test("an unwritten key is refused as not-uploaded, not as a server error", function () {
    if (res.status === 422) {
      expect(["TX_IMG_004", "TX_IMG_005"]).to.include(res.body.code)
    }
  })

  test("a successful attach returns the dish showing its new photo", function () {
    if (res.status === 200) {
      expect(res.body.data.uid).to.equal(bru.getVar("admin_item_uid"))
      expect(res.body.data.image_url).to.be.a("string").and.not.equal("")
    }
  })
}

docs {
  # Attach an uploaded photo to the dish

**Step two of two, and the only step that checks anything.** A presigned PUT proves the client
sent the length and content type it promised; it says nothing about whether those bytes are a
photograph. Everything the signature cannot constrain is constrained here, against the object
that actually landed:

| Check | Failure |
| --- | --- |
| Key is well-formed **and names this restaurant and this dish** | `422 TX_IMG_005` |
| Object exists in the bucket | `422 TX_IMG_004` |
| Object is not zero bytes | `422 TX_IMG_004` |
| Size is within `storage.max_upload_bytes` | `413 TX_IMG_003` |
| Leading bytes really are a JPEG/PNG/WebP, **and match what R2 will serve it as** | `422 TX_IMG_006` |

The ownership check is the load-bearing one. The key arrives from the client, so being
well-formed is not enough -- a well-formed key naming **another restaurant** is exactly what
would be sent to point a dish at somebody else's object. Both uids in the key must match the
authenticated actor's restaurant and the dish in the path, or nothing touches the bucket
(`TX_IMG_005`, which is also what a malformed key gets: distinguishing the two would tell a
caller probing keys which guess was structurally right).

Rejected objects are deleted rather than left in the bucket.

On success the dish's previous photo is deleted **after** the row is updated, never before --
deleting first and then failing the write would leave the row pointing at bytes that no longer
exist. `image_url` is cleared alongside, so a dish cannot end up with both an uploaded photo and
a pasted URL.

A `422` here in a sequential collection run is correct: `upload-image-url` minted a key, but
Bruno did not PUT a file to it.
}
